-## Subtopics
-
-### MaskGaussian Pipeline
-The broader framework where Masked-Rasterization is used [S1]. It formulates pruning based on existence probability and uses the rasterization technique to dynamically assess Gaussian importance [S1]. It is chosen when dynamic Gaussian survival assessment is required without permanent attribute modification [S1]. [MaskGaussian](https://doi.org/10.1109/cvpr52734.2025.00072)
-
-### Probabilistic Pruning
-The method of sampling masks based on existence probability [S1]. This allows for diverse selection of pruned Gaussians over time [S1]. It is chosen when maintaining a diverse pool of candidate Gaussians is necessary to prevent premature convergence [S1]. [MaskGaussian](https://doi.org/10.1109/cvpr52734.2025.00072)
+*   **Zero Alpha Filtering:** If a Gaussian's opacity or scale is set to zero, its $\alpha_i$ becomes zero. Standard rasterizers filter these out *before* the blending loop. This prevents them from receiving any gradient updates, causing them to become "outdated" and unable to adjust their existence probability [S1]. Masked-rasterization must ensure that Gaussians with non-zero $\alpha_i$ but zero mask are still processed in the blending loop to receive mask gradients [S1].
+*   **Gradient Isolation:** It is critical that masked Gaussians do *not* receive gradients for their geometric attributes (scale, rotation, position) if they are effectively "off". The source specifies that masked splats do not receive gradients for $\alpha_i$ or normal attributes, only for the mask $m_i$ [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Similar to standard 3DGS rasterization, as the masking operation is a simple multiplication per splat per pixel [G]. The overhead is negligible [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing an additional mask value per Gaussian [S1].
-- **Performance:** Achieves pruning ratios of 62.4% (Mip-NeRF360), 67.7% (Tanks & Temples), and 75.3% (Deep Blending) with speedups of 2.05×, 2.19×, and 3.16× respectively, with only a 0.02 PSNR decline on the first two datasets [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Prevents "stale" Gaussians from becoming outdated by allowing them to receive gradients for their existence probability [S1]. Superior to multiplying masks with opacity/scale [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Requires careful implementation to ensure gradients do not flow to geometric attributes for masked Gaussians [S1].
-- **When to use:** Use when dynamic Gaussian pruning is needed and gradient flow to existence probability must be preserved [S1].
-- **When not to use:** Avoid when strict attribute isolation is impossible to guarantee or when memory bandwidth is severely constrained by additional mask storage [G].
+*   **Performance:** The method enables pruning over 60% of Gaussians on average with only a 0.02 PSNR decline [S1]. It achieves rendering speedups of $2.05\times$, $2.19\times$, and $3.16\times$ on Mip-NeRF360, Tanks & Temples, and Deep Blending datasets, respectively [S1].
+*   **Quality:** Substantially outperforms previous pruning methods that multiply masks with Gaussian attributes (opacity/scale), which lead to drastic quality decreases due to gradient starvation [S1].
+*   **Memory:** No significant additional memory overhead compared to standard 3DGS, as the mask is a single scalar per Gaussian.
 
 ## Background
-Masked-Rasterization was introduced in the **MaskGaussian** paper by researchers at Shanghai AI Laboratory, presented at CVPR 2025 [S1]. It addresses the limitations of previous pruning methods (e.g., [4, 17, 34]) that multiplied masks with Gaussian attributes, which caused zero alpha filtering and blocked gradient flow [S1]. The method models Gaussians as probabilistic entities, allowing for dynamic assessment of their contribution to the evolving scene [S1].
+**Origin:** Introduced in "MaskGaussian: Adaptive 3D Gaussian Representation from Probabilistic Masks" (CVPR 2025) by researchers from Shanghai AI Laboratory [S1].
+**History:** Previous pruning methods [4, 17, 34] failed to address gradient starvation by multiplying masks with scales or opacities, which resulted in zero density alpha and prevented gradient updates [S1]. MaskGaussian addresses this by modeling Gaussians as probabilistic entities and using masked-rasterization to allow unused but probabilistically existing Gaussians to receive gradients [S1].
 
